186569933 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186569806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569933 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569933 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569933 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186569771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569933 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d7d4f7d2-ef86-5eb2-85dd-e2f3c8149faa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569955 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186569851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569955 #edb/ref 1069 "q-174425" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569955 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569955 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the disjoint union of the sets ${A}_{1}=\\{3,6\\}$ and ${A}_{2}=\\{4,6\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569955 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "02e046ed-8ad2-55e9-9c98-624e899595b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569955 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the sets are overlapping since\n\n$$\n\\{3,6\\}\\cap \\{4,6\\}=\\{6\\}\\ne ∅.\n$$\n\nSo, for each of our sets, we form the associated set, where each element is tagged with the corresponding index, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {A}_{1} &  & \\,\\to \\, &  & {A}_{1}^{∗}=\\{(3,1),(6,1)\\} \\\\  & {A}_{2} &  & \\,\\to \\, &  & {A}_{2}^{∗}=\\{(4,2),(6,2)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the disjoint union of our sets is simply the union of the associated tagged sets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2} & =\\{3,6\\}⊔\\{4,6\\} \\\\  & ={A}_{1}^{∗}\\cup {A}_{2}^{∗} \\\\  & =\\{(3,1),(6,1)\\}\\cup \\{(4,2),(6,2)\\} \\\\  & =\\{(3,1),(6,1),(4,2),(6,2)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569959 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186569933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569959 #edb/ref 1069 "q-174354" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569959 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569959 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following pairs of sets are disjoint?\n\nI. $\\{x,y,z\\}$ and $\\{3,6,9\\}$\n\nII. $\\{a,b,c,d,e\\}$ and $\\{a,e,i,o,u\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{1,3,6\\}$ and $\\{3,5,7\\}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569959 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0803d7dd-fe65-5059-8e1e-9052cd416dca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569959 #edb/ref 1074 "Two sets $A$ and $B$ are *disjoint* if they have no element in common, i.e., their intersection is empty:\n\n$$\nA\\cap B=∅\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets.\n\n- Pair I is disjoint. Indeed, the intersection of the sets is empty:\n\n$$\n\\{x,y,z\\}\\cap \\{3,6,9\\}=∅\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Pair II is *not* disjoint since the intersection of the sets is not empty:\n\n$$\n\\{a,b,c,d,e\\}\\cap \\{a,e,i,o,u\\}=\\{a,e\\}\\ne ∅\\,\\,\\times\n$$\n\n- Pair III is *not* disjoint since the intersection of the sets is not empty:\n\n$$\n\\{1,3,6\\}\\cap \\{3,5,7\\}=\\{3\\}\\ne ∅\\,\\,\\times\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569979 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186569853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569979 #edb/ref 1069 "q-174364" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569979 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569979 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nA=\\{{n}^{2}∣n\\in N\\},\\,B=\\{\\frac{2}{n}∣n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A=\\{1,4,9,16,…\\}$\n\nII. $A\\cap B=∅$\n\nIII. $A$ and $B$ are disjoint" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569979 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "37c3bc23-bd02-5d58-b8ef-2de055e363b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569979 #edb/ref 1074 "Two sets $A$ and $B$ are *disjoint* if they have no element in common, i.e., their intersection is empty:\n\n$$\nA\\cap B=∅\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets.\n\n- Statement I is true.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\{{n}^{2}∣n\\in N\\} \\\\  & =\\{1,4,9,16,…\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =\\{\\frac{2}{n}∣n\\in N\\} \\\\  & =\\{2,1,\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{2},…\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the intersection is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cap B & =\\{1,4,9,16,…\\}\\text{ }\\cap \\{2,1,\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{2},…\\} \\\\  & =\\{1\\}. \\\\  & \\ne ∅.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the intersection is not empty, the two sets are not disjoint.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569986 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186569856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569986 #edb/ref 1069 "q-174361" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569986 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569986 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nA=\\{n\\in N∣n\\le 5\\},\\,B=\\{n\\in N∣4n+6\\ge 30\\}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $B=\\{6,7,8,…\\}$\n\nII. $A\\cap B=∅$\n\nIII. $A$ and $B$ are disjoint" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569986 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4e68e4d1-4fbe-504a-90e3-9d792c948b3d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569986 #edb/ref 1074 "Two sets $A$ and $B$ are *disjoint* if they have no element in common, i.e., their intersection is empty:\n\n$$\nA\\cap B=∅\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the elements of $B$ are positive integers that satisfy the inequality \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4n+6 & \\ge 30 \\\\ 4n & \\ge 24 \\\\ n & \\ge 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets.\n\n- Statement I is true.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =\\{n\\in N∣4n+6\\ge 30\\} \\\\  & =\\{n\\in N∣n\\ge 6\\} \\\\  & =\\{6,7,8,…\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\{n\\in N∣n\\le 5\\} \\\\  & =\\{1,2,3,4,5\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the intersection is \n\n$$\nA\\cap B=\\{1,2,3,4,5\\}\\cap \\{6,7,8,…\\}=∅.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the intersection is empty, the two sets are disjoint.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569990 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186569860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569990 #edb/ref 1069 "q-174400" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569990 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569990 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\{-6,6\\}⊔\\{-3,3\\}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569990 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "568ef841-bdd5-5e7a-98b5-6d308da6308f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569990 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the sets are disjoint since\n\n$$\n\\{-6,6\\}\\cap \\{-3,3\\}=∅.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the disjoint union is simply the union:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{-6,6\\}⊔\\{-3,3\\} & =\\{-6,6\\}\\cup \\{-3,3\\} \\\\  & =\\{-6,-3,3,6\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
